<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanchipuram</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanchi, Conjeevaram, Conjeeveram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanchipuram is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Vegavathi river, counted among the seven sacred cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. It was the capital of the Pallava dynasty from about the fourth to the ninth century and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Buddhist and Jain learning, and later an important city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and Vijayanagara. Its many temples include the Kailasanatha and Vaikuntha Perumal, and it is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanchipuram.jpg" alt="Kanchipuram" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sridhar.selvaraj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parameswara_Vinnagaram.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
